    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d306-a1">

                <pb facs="0412" n="332" xml:id="pg332"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1.— Translation.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Viscount Aoki to
                            Minister of France.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1900-02-26"><hi rendition="#italic">February 26,
                        1900</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Monsieur le Ministre:</hi> I have examined the letter
                    which your excellency did me the honor to write me on the 9th of the current
                    month on the subject of the title to lease in perpetuity. As I told you in my
                    letter of the 3d, the provisions of the civil code which may be in conflict with
                    those of the treaties are, naturally, not applied to the title to lease in
                    perpetuity. The provision which, in article 268 of the civil code, limits the
                    titles of holders of superficies, it goes without saying, and, as a general
                    rule, all the provisions which imply conditions other than those which are
                    written in the titles to leases in perpetuity, do not apply to these.</p>
                <p>Accept, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Vt. Aoki.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
